{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Do not come near, etc. Put off, etc.</b> Behold, HaShem commands him two things: one, that he not come near, and one, that he remove the shoe of his foot, so that he not stand in it even in the place where he is standing, as it says <b>the place where you stand</b> is already <b>holy ground</b>. And there is a fine point to examine: why did He not first tell him to remove the stumbling block that already applied to him — since he was standing with his shoes on holy ground — and only afterward command him not to come here?" "Know that in the entire Torah, HaShem revealed His knowledge and His will, that the essence of His strictness and His reproof concerns negative commandments (לא תעשה), for these blemish the soul. And this is the reason for His saying (Ecclesiastes 12:7): ״וְהָרוּחַ תָּשׁוּב וְגוֹ׳ אֲשֶׁר נְתָנָהּ״, and they said, of blessed memory (Shabbat 152b): give it back to Him as He gave it to you. But positive commandments (עשה) are the attainment of good when they are performed, and in their absence there is no punishment, except for specific known cases such as Pesach and circumcision." "And behold, here HaShem commanded him a negative commandment and a positive commandment. The negative commandment is <b>do not come here</b>, and they said of blessed memory (Eiruvin 96a; Shevuot 36b): wherever ״פֶּן״ or ״אַל״ is stated, it is nothing but a negative commandment. And the positive commandment is <b>put off your shoes.</b> Now if Moses were to come here, he would transgress a negative commandment; and if he did not remove his shoe, even though it is not respectful toward the Place, he would transgress only a positive commandment, from what HaShem said to him in the language of a positive command. And HaShem always gives precedence to guarding over doing, as it is written (Deuteronomy 4:6): ״וּשְׁמַרְתֶּם וַעֲשִׂיתֶם״, ״לִשְׁמֹר לַעֲשׂוֹת״ (Deuteronomy 15:5), ״מִשְׁמַרְתִּי מִצְוֹתַי״ (Genesis 26:5). Therefore He also gave precedence to the weightier <b>do not come near</b> — the negative commandment — and afterward the positive commandment <b>put off</b>, etc., that he conduct himself with respect in the holy place." "But we still need to know the supernal knowledge: why did He not call to him before he would stand in a holy place, before he would come near with his shoe that was on his foot on holy ground. Perhaps it is because the matter was not fitting for the divine speech to be addressed to him except together with the warning of <b>do not come here.</b> Further, it is possible to say that the place where he stood was not sanctified until afterward, and the reason is that when HaShem wished to speak with him there in the place of his standing, He wished to sanctify it and warned him beforehand. Or perhaps this is why the verse was precise in saying the word הוּא, for it should have needed to say only 'the place, etc., holy ground'; rather, the verse intended by saying <b>הוּא</b> to say: it was not so beforehand."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:23:57.280131826Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}